Subject: [PATCH] samsung-sit: bind the Google Kleaf CPIF SIT ports via sit-proxy

Target-local patch for the Pixel 9a (google-pixel-9a-tegu-gxq96-mp1-0) on
the Google Kleaf CPIF kernel route (route A, gate S3). See
targets/boards/google-pixel-9a-tegu-gxq96-mp1-0/s5300-s3-mm-binding.md.

The pinned CPIF driver (a8976934) creates the SIT FMT channels 245/246 as
umts_ipc0/umts_ipc1 in the "cpif" device class with no parent device, and
its rmnet* netdevs with no parent either. Before this patch the fork only
binds "wwan" SIT ports of the s5300-modem driver, so no modem is created on
CPIF. This patch:

- kernel device (udev): when a parentless device reports no driver, take it
  from the udev property ID_MM_KERNEL_DRIVER;
- plugin core: probe "cpif" ports only when udev declared them SIT (their
  probe results are preset, so nothing is sent), and grab them as SIT ports;
- samsung-sit plugin: also watch "cpif" and accept the cp_interface driver;
  ID_MM_SIT_PROXY_REQUIRED makes sit-proxy the only transport (no fallback
  to opening the device node);
- sit client: map umts_ipcN to /run/sit-proxy/N (it mapped every CPIF name
  to socket 0), with unit tests.

No SIT protocol behaviour changes.

diff -ruN a/src/kerneldevice/mm-kernel-device-udev.c b/src/kerneldevice/mm-kernel-device-udev.c
--- a/src/kerneldevice/mm-kernel-device-udev.c	2026-11-25 10:49:57
+++ b/src/kerneldevice/mm-kernel-device-udev.c	2026-11-25 10:49:57
@@ -83,6 +83,19 @@
      * together. Also, obviously, no vendor, product, revision or interface. */
     self->priv->driver = g_strdup (g_udev_device_get_driver (self->priv->device));
 
+    /* A class device the kernel created without a parent (device_create() with
+     * a NULL parent, or a netdev without SET_NETDEV_DEV(), as Google's CPIF
+     * does for its umts_ipc* nodes and rmnet* netdevs) has no driver at all,
+     * and plugins filter on drivers. Let udev name the driver that owns it,
+     * but only when the kernel reports none. */
+    if (!self->priv->driver) {
+        const gchar *driver;
+
+        driver = g_udev_device_get_property (self->priv->device, "ID_MM_KERNEL_DRIVER");
+        if (driver && driver[0])
+            self->priv->driver = g_strdup (driver);
+    }
+
     /* But look for a lower real physical device, as we may have one */
     lower_device_name = mm_kernel_device_get_lower_device_name (g_udev_device_get_sysfs_path (self->priv->device));
     if (lower_device_name) {
